# Proposed NIDM-Experiment vocabulary entry for a field-map image-usage type.
#
# WHERE THIS GOES: the NIDM-Experiment vocabulary rendered at
#   https://incf-nidash.github.io/nidm-experiment/index.html
# (maintained externally). PyNIDM only EMITS the URI nidm:FieldMap as an
# nidm:hadImageUsageType value; the term itself must be defined in the
# vocabulary source so it is documented and dereferenceable.
#
# CONTEXT FROM THE CURRENT VOCABULARY:
#   * There is a parent class  nidm:'Image Usage Type'  (local name
#     ImageUsageType) whose existing children include: Anatomical, Functional
#     (-> Resting State, Task Based), Diffusion Tensor, Localization, and
#     DistortionCorrection.
#   * There is NO field-map usage type yet.
#   * NOTE the existing sibling  nidm:DistortionCorrection : a field map is
#     often acquired *for* distortion correction, so the maintainer may prefer
#     to (a) add nidm:FieldMap as below, (b) reuse nidm:DistortionCorrection,
#     or (c) make FieldMap a child of DistortionCorrection.  Whichever URI the
#     vocabulary settles on, the bidsmri2nidm tools will emit that exact URI
#     (they currently emit nidm:FieldMap).
#
# The block below mirrors how the sibling usage types (Anatomical, Functional)
# are modeled: an owl:Class that is rdfs:subClassOf nidm:'Image Usage Type'.

@prefix nidm: <http://purl.org/nidash/nidm#> .
@prefix owl:  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .

nidm:FieldMap
    a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Field Map" ;
    rdfs:subClassOf nidm:ImageUsageType ;
    rdfs:comment
        "Field Map is a description of the usage of a given acquisition object, indicating that the object is a magnetic-field (B0) map used to characterize and correct susceptibility-induced geometric distortion in MRI (typically of EPI-based functional or diffusion scans). Corresponds to the BIDS field-map data type (fmap/) and its suffixes: fieldmap, magnitude, magnitude1, magnitude2, phasediff, phase1, phase2, and epi." ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy nidm: .
